  "text": "Question nine. Explain dropout, L2, and early stopping as regularizers. Dropout zeroes a random share of units each training step, commonly a fifth to a half, so none can rely on particular others. Survivors are usually scaled up in training, so at inference it's simply off. Leave it on in a digit classifier trained with it. Ten calls on one image of an eight: eight say eight, at forty-eight to ninety-three percent, and two say one. Across the test set, one image in ten changes its answer within ten calls. Yet accuracy only slips from ninety-eight to ninety-seven percent, so no dashboard flags it. Check that serving runs in evaluation mode, and that one input gets one output. L2 penalizes squared weights, pulling them toward zero. With plain SGD that's the same as weight decay; with Adam it isn't, so decay the weights directly. Early stopping keeps the weights from the best validation epoch, and all three limit what a network can memorize. Then, architectures shaped to their data.",
